content='<think>\nAlright, I\'m trying to figure out how to create an Ansible Playbook based on the user\'s instructions. Let\'s break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, the user specified that the playbook must start with \'---\' on the very first line. So, I know the playbook should begin with that.\n\nNext, the playbook needs to be valid YAML and conform to yamllint and ansible-lint standards. That means I have to ensure proper indentation and syntax, like using the correct YAML tags and structures.\n\nThe playbook should be structured to be included directly in an Ansible Role under the tasks/ directory, specifically tasks/main.yml. So, I should organize the tasks in the correct order and use the right modules as specified.\n\nThere are three tasks to include:\n\n1. **Task 1**: Enable Backports repository for Debian versions older than 9. I\'ll use the apt_repository module. The repository details need to be set correctly, and the filename should be based on the distribution release. Also, I need to update the package cache after adding the repo.\n\n2. **Task 2**: Set the default_release fact. For older Debian versions, I have to append "-backports" to the ansible_distribution_release fact and store it as ansible_default_release.\n\n3. **Task 3**: Install Ansible using the apt module. The state parameter should come from ansible_package_state, and the default_release should use the fact set in Task 2.\n\nI need to make sure each task is correctly ordered and uses the right modules and parameters. Also, I must not include any hosts, vars, or playbook-level headers since it\'s meant to be part of a role.\n\nI\'ll start by writing the first task with apt_repository, ensuring the repo URL is correct and the state is present. Then, I\'ll update the package cache.\n\nFor the second task, I\'ll use set_fact to create ansible_default_release based on the distribution release.\n\nFinally, the third task will use apt to install the package, referencing the state and default_release fact.\n\nI should also check that all tasks are valid and that the playbook starts with \'---\' followed by the tasks. I need to avoid any comments or extra formatting beyond the YAML structure.\n</think>\n\n```yaml\n---\n- name: Enable Backports repository for Debian distributions older than version 9\n  apt_repository:\n    repo: "deb http://backports.debian.org/debian-backports {{ ansible_distribution_version }} main"\n    state: present\n    filename: "{{ ansible_distribution_release }}-backports"\n\n- name: Set the default_release option for older Debian versions\n  set_fact:\n    ansible_default_release: "{{ ansible_distribution_release }}-backports"\n\n- name: Install Ansible using the default_release set for older Debian versions\n  apt:\n    name: ansible\n    state: present\n    default_release: "{{ ansible_default_release }}"\n    force: yes\n```' additional_kwargs={} response_metadata={'model': 'deepseek-r1:14b', 'created_at': '2025-10-08T08:33:08.412680273Z', 'done': True, 'done_reason': 'stop', 'total_duration': 10123403042, 'load_duration': 102794643, 'prompt_eval_count': 401, 'prompt_eval_duration': 97714507, 'eval_count': 595, 'eval_duration': 9921374375, 'model_name': 'deepseek-r1:14b'} id='run--8d84d338-c2a8-4c01-be1a-4a79faab48c9-0' usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 401, 'output_tokens': 595, 'total_tokens': 996}
